On Sat, 17 Oct 2026 03:44:54 -0700, Pat Maddox wrote:
> How is it currently configured, to prevent it from working on its own
> files?

There are two guards. Only the jail is enforced.

1. The jail. lab-claude mounts /jails/share/lab.git read-only and
   lab-submissions.git read-write
   (jails/instances/lab-claude/jail.conf). Your checkouts on the host
   are never visible to the agent. That is the only hard boundary.

2. Separate jj workspaces inside the jail. lab-process-mail runs from
   an installed copy in ~/local, with prompt.md shipped beside it, so
   a pass cannot edit the loop that is running it ("[PATCH 1/5]
   src/lab-process-mail: Answer the inbox from outside the tree" [1]).
   Each pass works in its own workspace, work.jj, apart from
   default.jj and mail.jj ("[PATCH 4/5] src/lab-process-mail: Run
   each pass in its own workspace" [2]).

The second guard is weak in three ways:

- All three workspaces belong to one repo, and the only thing keeping
  the agent out of default.jj is the line "Leave its working copy
  alone" in prompt.md. The pass runs with --permission-mode auto, so
  nothing technical stops it.
- prompt.md has the agent run ${ws}/libexec/mail/format-patch.sh and
  maildir-deliver.sh from the same workspace it is editing. A
  half-done change to those scripts is the copy that posts the
  series. This is the clearest case of lab operating on itself.
- The paths are hardcoded to the lab repo:
  ~/lab/{default,work,mail}.jj in lab-process-mail (overridable by
  TRUNK_WORKSPACE, WORK_WORKSPACE, and MAIL_WORKSPACE), the same paths
  as literals in prompt.md, /data/lab.git in the lab-claude
  bootstrap.sh, and the notmuch hook_dir under default.jj. The list
  is the `mail` bookmark of that same repo, too.

> I think it needs some idea of repo resources that it can fetch -
> it just so happens that it can also work on itself (in a different
> checkout).

I agree. Nothing on the list defines this yet; the nearest earlier
sketch is my reply in "[RFC] designing a yolo box" [3]. Here is what I
propose.

A repo resource is a name plus what the tooling needs to fetch and
deliver it:

    name = lab
    upstream = /data/lab.git (read-only)
    submissions = /data/lab-submissions.git
    base = trunk()
    mail = mail@upstream (where its list lives)

The tools (lab-process-mail, libexec/mail, and the notmuch hooks)
ship as one installed package and never run from a checkout of a
resource. A pass fetches the resources it needs into fresh checkouts
it owns and works there. Lab is then one entry among many. Working on
lab means checking out the lab resource, much as you would check out
freebsd-ports, and that checkout has no bearing on the copy of the
tools that is running.

> Consider that the way I'm working with lab, I would also want to work
> with any other number of repos.

This fits the yolo box direction you set in [3]: one dataset per repo,
a clone per run, and a jail started with whatever repos the run needs.
A resource list is the input that tool takes.

I would take it in three small series, each useful by itself:

1. Install libexec/mail with lab-process-mail and make prompt.md call
   the installed scripts, not ${ws}/libexec/mail. This closes the
   self-editing hole now and costs little: one Makefile/Justfile
   install rule and four lines of prompt.md.
2. Replace the three *_WORKSPACE variables and the literals in
   prompt.md and bootstrap.sh with one resource file per repo, e.g.
   ~/.config/lab/repos/lab.conf, read by lab-process-mail and passed
   to the prompt as variables. The prompt then names no path at all.
3. Let a pass take more than one resource, with each checkout created
   from its resource and dropped after the pass. This is where it
   meets the yolo box work, and it waits on your answer there about
   the trust model.

I recommend starting with 1, since it is independent of the design
questions. Two things I need from you before 2:

- Should the list stay a bookmark inside each repo (one list per
  repo), or become its own resource that several repos share? Today
  the code assumes the former, but lab-devel already carries work
  for jails and ports that isn't lab's own code.
- Where should the resource files live: in the tools package, in
  the jail's dist/ tree, or in a repo of their own?

